WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.460
Post-quantum migration has real engineering consequences.

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:06.455
Some algorithms use larger keys, larger signatures,

00:00:06.455 --> 00:00:08.660
or larger messages than teams are used to handling.

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:13.450
That can affect bandwidth, storage, packet sizes, certificate chains, handshake behavior,

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:17.160
logging volume, database columns, message queues, and embedded protocols.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:18.955
Even when the cryptography is correct,

00:00:18.955 --> 00:00:23.925
surrounding systems may assume older sizes or timing patterns and fail in unexpected ways.

00:00:23.925 --> 00:00:26.330
Performance must be measured in the environment that matters.

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:31.155
A developer workstation benchmark is useful, but it is not the same as a busy API gateway,

00:00:31.155 --> 00:00:35.185
a constrained device, a high-volume signing service, a hardware-backed key operation,

00:00:35.185 --> 00:00:37.060
or a remote site with limited bandwidth.

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:41.390
Teams should test latency, CPU, memory, concurrency, certificate validation paths,

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:45.020
key generation, signing throughput, and failure modes under realistic load.

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:47.930
Legacy and embedded systems are often the hardest part.

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:51.800
Industrial devices, network appliances, fielded products, long-lived firmware,

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:55.060
and older operating systems may not support new algorithms quickly.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:57.865
Cloud and SaaS dependencies introduce another layer:

00:00:57.865 --> 00:01:01.160
customers may have to wait for provider support, review roadmap claims,

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:04.960
update integrations, and verify that configuration options are actually available.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:08.425
Procurement and vendor management become part of the technical migration.

00:01:08.425 --> 00:01:11.230
Operations teams also need updated observability.

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:15.660
Monitoring, alerts, asset inventory, vulnerability management, configuration review,

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:19.675
and incident response playbooks may need to understand new protocol options and failure

00:01:19.675 --> 00:01:20.335
states.

00:01:20.335 --> 00:01:24.810
A strong program involves architecture, engineering, security, operations, procurement,

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:26.675
compliance, and vendor management.

00:01:26.675 --> 00:01:29.550
PQC adoption is not just choosing an algorithm;

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:32.155
it is making sure the whole system can run, be monitored,

00:01:32.155 --> 00:01:33.910
and be supported after the change.
